= 1
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

Use <completing the square> after taking out the leading <coefficient>:
$$
3(x^2-4x)+11=3[(x-2)^2-4]+11
=\boxed{3(x-2)^2-1.}
$$
Thus $a=3$, $b=-2$ and $c=-1$. Since a real square is nonnegative, the <global minimum> is **$-1$, attained at $x=2$**.

= 2
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

The <derivative> of the <parabola> is $dy/dx=2+2x$, giving <tangent line> slope $4$ at $(1,3)$. A <normal line to a plane curve> has the negative reciprocal slope, since its direction is <orthogonal> to a <tangent vector>. Hence
$$
\boxed{y-3=-\frac14(x-1),\qquad x+4y=13.}
$$
The point $(1,3)$ satisfies the equation, and the product of the two slopes is $-1$.

= 3
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

By the <polynomial remainder theorem>, the first <polynomial remainder> is $f(1)=23-2p$. Equating it to $9$ gives **$p=7$**.

For the separate <polynomial long division>, successively cancel the leading powers of the dividend. Subtracting $x^2(x-1)$ leaves $2x^2+2x+1$; subtracting $2x(x-1)$ leaves $4x+1$; subtracting $4(x-1)$ leaves $5$. Thus
$$
x^3+x^2+2x+1=(x-1)(x^2+2x+4)+5,
\qquad\boxed{\text{quotient }x^2+2x+4,\quad\text{remainder }5.}
$$
The <polynomial remainder> also equals the dividend evaluated at $x=1$.

= 4
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Apply the <product rule> and the <chain rule> to the <exponential function>:
$$
\boxed{\frac d{dx}(xe^{-2x})=e^{-2x}-2xe^{-2x}=(1-2x)e^{-2x}.}
$$

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

On the real <domain of a function> $x>0$, the <quotient rule> gives
$$
\boxed{\frac d{dx}\left(\frac{\ln x}{x}\right)
=\frac{x(1/x)-\ln x}{x^2}=\frac{1-\ln x}{x^2}.}
$$

= 5
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

Use <integration by parts> twice. First,
$$
I=[-x^2\cos x]_0^\pi+2\int_0^\pi x\cos x\,dx
=\pi^2+2\big([x\sin x]_0^\pi-\int_0^\pi\sin x\,dx\big).
$$
The bracket containing $x\sin x$ vanishes, while the last <definite integral> is $2$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{I=\pi^2-4.}
$$

= 6
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

A repeated linear factor requires both reciprocal powers. Write the <partial fraction decomposition> as
$$
\frac{A}{x-1}+\frac{B}{(x-1)^2}+\frac{C}{x+2}.
$$
Multiplication by the common denominator gives $4x^2-x=A(x-1)(x+2)+B(x+2)+C(x-1)^2$. Setting $x=1$ gives $B=1$, and setting $x=-2$ gives $C=2$. Comparing the coefficients of $x^2$ then gives $A=2$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\frac{4x^2-x}{(x-1)^2(x+2)}
=\frac2{x-1}+\frac1{(x-1)^2}+\frac2{x+2},\qquad x\ne1,-2.}
$$

= 7
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=7}

For a nonzero solution, the <separable differential equation> gives $dy/y=3x^2dx$. Integrating yields $\ln|y|=x^3+C$, hence $y=Ke^{x^3}$. The <initial condition> fixes $K=3/e$, so
$$
\boxed{y(x)=3e^{x^3-1},\qquad x\in\mathbb R.}
$$
The zero solution omitted by division by $y$ cannot satisfy the given <initial condition>. Differentiating the displayed solution verifies $y'=3x^2y$.

