= Solution
{parent=a}

This <geometric series> has first term $2^{-4}=1/16$ and common ratio $1/2$, whose <absolute value> is less than one. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n=4}^\infty2^{-n}=\frac{1/16}{1-1/2}=\frac18.}
$$

= b
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <binomial theorem> assigns <binomial coefficient> $\binom93$ to $x^3$. Since $\binom93=9\cdot8\cdot7/(3\cdot2\cdot1)=84$, the requested term is **$84x^3$**.

= 9
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <domain of a function> is $\mathbb R\setminus\{0\}$, because the denominator vanishes at zero. Near zero, $e^x-1\sim x$, so $x=0$ is a vertical <asymptote>, approached with value $-\infty$ from the left and $+\infty$ from the right. The horizontal <asymptotes> are $y=-1$ as $x\to-\infty$ and $y=0$ as $x\to+\infty$.

Moreover,
$$
f'(x)=-\frac{e^x}{(e^x-1)^2}<0.
$$
Both branches define a strictly decreasing <monotone function>: the left branch lies below $-1$, and the right branch is positive. There are no zeros or vertical-axis intercept.

\Image[paper-1-exponential-reciprocal.png]
{title=The decreasing reciprocal-exponential branches with their three asymptotes}

= b
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <absolute value> in the original PDF reflects every negative <sine> arch upward. The graph is nonnegative, with zeros at $x=k\pi$ for $k=0,1,2,3,4$, and it lies below the decaying envelope $e^{-x}$. On the $k$th arch, <differentiation> gives a factor proportional to $\cos x-\sin x$. Thus its unique <local maximum> is
$$
x_k=\frac\pi4+k\pi,\qquad
f(x_k)=\frac{e^{-(\pi/4+k\pi)}}{\sqrt2},\qquad k=0,1,2,3.
$$
Successive maximum heights have ratio $e^{-\pi}$. At each interior zero the one-sided slopes have opposite signs, producing a corner. The smaller panels below magnify the later arches so that all four remain visible.

\Image[paper-1-damped-absolute-sine.png]
{title=Four positive exponentially damped sine arches, with separate linear-scale enlargements of the later arches}

= 10
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=10}

A cross-section perpendicular to the <rotation> axis is a <disk> of <radius> $\sin x$. <Disc integration> therefore gives
$$
V=\pi\int_0^\pi\sin^2x\,dx
=\frac\pi2\int_0^\pi(1-\cos2x)\,dx
=\boxed{\frac{\pi^2}{2}}.
$$
The result is in cubic coordinate units; the full <rotation> is already accounted for by the disk <area>.

= 11
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Collecting the coefficients gives the <coefficient matrix>
$$
\boxed{A=\begin{pmatrix}a&0&2\\0&b+1&c-1\\0&c+1&b-1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
A\begin{pmatrix}x\\y\\z\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}0\\0\\0\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
A square <homogeneous linear system> has a nonzero solution precisely when its <matrix> is a <singular matrix>. Expanding the <determinant> down the first column,
$$
\det A=a[(b+1)(b-1)-(c-1)(c+1)]=a(b^2-c^2).
$$
Hence the necessary and sufficient condition is **$a=0$ or $b=c$ or $b=-c$**. A zero <determinant> gives a nontrivial <kernel of a linear map>; otherwise multiplication by the <matrix inverse> would force the zero solution.

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

With $a=1$, $b=2$, $c=2$, substitute into the matrix equations. The equations reduce to $x+2z=0$ and $3y+z=0$, with the latter repeated. Taking $z=3t$ gives the complete <null space>
$$
\boxed{(x,y,z)=t(-6,-1,3),\qquad t\in\mathbb R.}
$$
The two independent rows impose two constraints on three unknowns, leaving exactly one free parameter. This also includes the zero solution at $t=0$.

= c
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Use <orthogonality to the image of a matrix>. For $n=(1,2,1)^T$, the condition is $n^TA=0$, since $n^TAr$ must vanish for every nonzero input. Direct multiplication gives
$$
n^TA=(a,\ 2b+c+3,\ b+2c-1).
$$
Each coefficient must vanish; choosing the three coordinate <basis vectors> as inputs already proves necessity. Solve $2b+c=-3$, $b+2c=1$ to obtain
$$
\boxed{a=0,\qquad b=-\frac73,\qquad c=\frac53.}
$$
These values make every output have zero <dot product> with $n$, including any zero output. Here <orthogonality> is understood in this algebraic sense; no direction is assigned to the zero <vector>.

= d
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For $n=(1,-1,1)^T$, <orthogonality to the image of a matrix> would require
$$
n^TA=(a,\ c-b,\ b-c+2)=0.
$$
The second component forces $c=b$, but then the third component is $2$. Equivalently, the last two required zero components always sum to $2$. Therefore **there are no such values of $a,b,c$**.

