= Solution
{parent=i}

Multiply numerator and denominator by the <complex conjugate> of the denominator:
$$
\frac{2+i}{-1+i}=\frac{(2+i)(-1-i)}2=\frac{-1-3i}{2}.
$$
Squaring gives $(-1-3i)^2/4=(-8+6i)/4$. Thus the <real part> and <imaginary part> are
$$
\boxed{\Re z=-2,\qquad\Im z=\frac32.}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

In <polar form of a complex number>, $1+i=\sqrt2\,e^{i\pi/4}$. By <De Moivre's theorem>,
$$
(1+i)^{10}=2^5e^{5\pi i/2}=32i,
\qquad\boxed{\Re z=0,\qquad\Im z=32.}
$$

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <complex sine function> identity $\sin(u+iv)=\sin u\cosh v+i\cos u\sinh v$ follows from the exponential definition of <sine>. With $u=\pi/2$, its imaginary term vanishes, while
$$
\cosh(\ln2)=\frac{e^{\ln2}+e^{-\ln2}}2=\frac{2+1/2}{2}=\frac54.
$$
Consequently **the <real part> is $5/4$ and the <imaginary part> is $0$**.

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write a <square root> as $u+iv$, with $u,v$ real. Equating the <real part> and <imaginary part> of its square gives $u^2-v^2=-1$ and $2uv=1$. Taking <complex modulus> also gives $u^2+v^2=\sqrt2$. Therefore
$$
u^2=\frac{\sqrt2-1}{2},\qquad v^2=\frac{\sqrt2+1}{2}.
$$
Since $uv>0$, the signs agree. The two roots are
$$
\boxed{\pm\left(\sqrt{\frac{\sqrt2-1}{2}}+i\sqrt{\frac{\sqrt2+1}{2}}\right).}
$$
The $\pm$ changes both signs together, rather than independently.

= c
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Set $z=e^{i\theta}$, so $2i\sin\theta=z-z^{-1}$. The <binomial theorem> gives
$$
(2i\sin\theta)^5
=(z^5-z^{-5})-5(z^3-z^{-3})+10(z-z^{-1}).
$$
Use $z^k-z^{-k}=2i\sin(k\theta)$ and $(2i)^5=32i$ to obtain
$$
\boxed{\sin^5\theta=\frac{10\sin\theta-5\sin3\theta+\sin5\theta}{16}.}
$$
This is a finite <Fourier sine series>, with only odd harmonics.

= d
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Using the exponential definition of <hyperbolic cosine>, multiply $e^z+e^{-z}=-2$ by the nonzero quantity $e^z$. This yields $(e^z+1)^2=0$, hence $e^z=-1$.

If $z=x+iy$, its <complex modulus> requires $e^x=1$, so $x=0$; its phase then requires $y=(2k+1)\pi$. Thus all complex solutions are
$$
\boxed{z=(2k+1)\pi i,\qquad k\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
There are no real solutions, since real $\cosh x\geq1$.

= 13
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Work on an <interval> with $x\ne0$. The <homogeneous first-order differential equation> becomes a <separable differential equation> under $v=y/x$: substituting $y=xv$ gives $v+xv'=v^2+v$, hence $xv'=v^2$.

For $v\ne0$, <integration> gives $-1/v=\ln|x|+C$. Renaming the arbitrary constant produces
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\frac{x}{C-\ln|x|},\qquad\text{or}\qquad y(x)\equiv0.}
$$
For the nonzero family the denominator must remain nonzero, so take any connected <interval> avoiding both $x=0$ and $|x|=e^C$. The zero solution must be restored after division by $v^2$; by the <Picard-Lindelöf theorem> away from $x=0$, a solution cannot switch between zero and a nonzero branch there.

= b
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $M=x+y$ and $N=x$. Their cross <partial derivatives> agree: $M_y=N_x=1$. More directly, construct the <scalar> <function>
$$
F(x,y)=\frac{x^2}{2}+xy,\qquad dF=(x+y)\,dx+x\,dy.
$$
This exhibits the expression as an <exact differential>. Along a solution of the <differential equation>,
$$
\frac d{dx}F(x,y(x))=x+y+xy'=0.
$$
Thus the <general solution> is
$$
\boxed{\frac{x^2}{2}+xy=C,\qquad y=\frac Cx-\frac x2\quad(x\ne0).}
$$
For $C=0$, the graph $y=-x/2$ also extends smoothly through $x=0$ and satisfies the original, undivided equation there. Nonzero $C$ cannot extend through zero as a finite-valued <differentiable> solution. The extra vertical component $x=0$ of the implicit zero-level equation is not a graph $y(x)$ on an <interval> and is not an additional solution of this <ordinary differential equation>.

= c
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <integrating factor> is $e^{kx}$, giving $(e^{kx}y)'=ae^{kx}\sin(mx)$. The required <antiderivative> is
$$
\int e^{kx}\sin(mx)\,dx
=\frac{e^{kx}}{k^2+m^2}[k\sin(mx)-m\cos(mx)].
$$
After dividing by $e^{kx}$, the <general solution> is the oscillatory term plus $Ce^{-kx}$. The <initial condition> fixes $C=1+am/(k^2+m^2)$, so
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\left(1+\frac{am}{k^2+m^2}\right)e^{-kx}
+\frac{a[k\sin(mx)-m\cos(mx)]}{k^2+m^2}.}
$$
The real, nonzero parameters ensure $k^2+m^2>0$; direct substitution verifies both the equation and $y(0)=1$.

= 14
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <vector triple product identity> is
$$
\boxed{a\times(b\times c)=b(a\cdot c)-c(a\cdot b).}
$$
Thus the expression lies in the <linear span> of $b$ and $c$, with respective <scalar> coefficients $a\cdot c$ and $-a\cdot b$. The order and the minus sign matter because the <cross product> is not an <associative operation>.

= b
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The nonzero <vectors> $b,c$ are <normal vectors> to the two <planes>. If $b\times c=0$, then $c=qb$ for some nonzero real $q$. Their equations are therefore $r\cdot b=\lambda$ and $q(r\cdot b)=\mu$.

When $\mu=q\lambda$, the <planes> coincide and their intersection is an entire <plane>. Otherwise they are distinct parallel planes and have no intersection. Neither case has exactly a <line> as its intersection. Hence **$b\times c\ne0$ is necessary** for intersection in a <line>. It means that the two <normal vectors> are not parallel; in that case it is also sufficient.

= c
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Apply the <vector triple product identity> and the two <plane> constraints:
$$
\boxed{r\times(b\times c)=b(r\cdot c)-c(r\cdot b)=\mu b-\lambda c.}
$$
Put $d=b\times c\ne0$. If $a$ is one intersection point, subtraction gives $(r-a)\times d=0$, so $r-a$ is parallel to $d$. Conversely, adding any multiple of $d$ preserves both <plane> constraints because $d$ is <orthogonal> to both <normal vectors>. The whole intersection is therefore
$$
\boxed{r=a+t(b\times c),\quad t\in\mathbb R,\qquad a\cdot b=\lambda,\quad a\cdot c=\mu.}
$$
Such an $a$ exists: choose $a=\alpha b+\beta c$ and solve the two-by-two <linear system> with <determinant> $|b|^2|c|^2-(b\cdot c)^2=|b\times c|^2>0$. This also proves sufficiency rather than assuming a nonempty intersection.

= d
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The squared <Euclidean distance> along the <line> is
$$
|a+td|^2=|a|^2+2t(a\cdot d)+t^2|d|^2,\qquad d=b\times c.
$$
Its minimum occurs at $t=0$ exactly when its <derivative> there vanishes, so **$a\cdot(b\times c)=0$**. Since $b,c$ are independent, their <linear span> is the two-dimensional <orthogonal complement> of $b\times c$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{a=\alpha b+\beta c}
$$
for unique real $\alpha,\beta$. This is the <closest point on the intersection of two planes>; the positive quadratic coefficient $|d|^2$ proves that the stationary distance is a strict <global minimum>.

= e
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

With $b\cdot c=0$, substitute $a=\alpha b+\beta c$ into the <plane> constraints. They become $\alpha|b|^2=\lambda$ and $\beta|c|^2=\mu$, giving
$$
\boxed{\alpha=\frac{\lambda}{|b|^2},\qquad\beta=\frac{\mu}{|c|^2}.}
$$
The <Pythagorean theorem> for the two <orthogonal> terms in $a$ then gives the closest distance
$$
\boxed{d_{\min}=|a|=\sqrt{\frac{\lambda^2}{|b|^2}+\frac{\mu^2}{|c|^2}}.}
$$
Zero values of either <scalar> are allowed, including zero distance if both planes contain the origin.

= 15
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Convert the <cardioid> from <polar coordinates> to <Cartesian coordinates>:
$$
x=(1+\cos\theta)\cos\theta,\qquad y=(1+\cos\theta)\sin\theta.
$$
The <curve> is symmetric about the horizontal axis. It has a <cusp> at the origin at $\theta=\pi$, passes through $(0,\pm1)$, and reaches $(2,0)$ on the right. Its leftmost horizontal coordinate is $-1/4$, since $x=(\cos\theta+1/2)^2-1/4$.

For the largest $y$, differentiate the parametrization:
$$
\frac{dy}{d\theta}=\cos\theta+\cos2\theta
=(2\cos\theta-1)(\cos\theta+1).
$$
The candidates have $\cos\theta=1/2$ or $-1$. The positive maximum occurs at $\theta=\pi/3$, where $r=3/2$. Comparing with the negative extremum and the zero <cusp> value proves
$$
\boxed{(x_M,y_M)=\left(\frac34,\frac{3\sqrt3}{4}\right).}
$$
\Image[paper-1-cardioid.png]
{title=The cardioid with its cusp, axis crossings and point of greatest vertical coordinate}

= b
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Each radial segment from $0$ to $1+\cos\theta$ lies inside the <cardioid>. Integrating the <polar area element> gives
$$
\mathcal A=\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^{1+\cos\theta}r\,dr\,d\theta
=\frac12\int_0^{2\pi}(1+2\cos\theta+\cos^2\theta)\,d\theta.
$$
The three integrals are respectively $2\pi$, $0$ and $\pi$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\mathcal A=\frac{3\pi}{2}}
$$
in square coordinate units, agreeing with the scale-one case of the <area and perimeter of a cardioid>.

= c
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Differentiate $x=r\cos\theta$, $y=r\sin\theta$:
$$
dx=\cos\theta\,dr-r\sin\theta\,d\theta,\qquad
dy=\sin\theta\,dr+r\cos\theta\,d\theta.
$$
Squaring and adding cancels the mixed terms and uses $\cos^2\theta+\sin^2\theta=1$, giving
$$
\boxed{dx^2+dy^2=dr^2+r^2d\theta^2.}
$$
For this <cardioid>, the <polar arc length> integrand is
$$
\sqrt{\sin^2\theta+(1+\cos\theta)^2}
=\sqrt{2+2\cos\theta}=2|\cos(\theta/2)|.
$$
The <cosine> inside the <absolute value> changes sign at $\theta=\pi$. Therefore the perimeter is
$$
\ell=2\int_0^\pi\cos(\theta/2)\,d\theta
-2\int_\pi^{2\pi}\cos(\theta/2)\,d\theta
=\boxed{8}
$$
in coordinate <length> units. The isolated <cusp> does not obstruct this piecewise <smooth> <arc length> integral.

= 16
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A <directional derivative> measures change per unit distance, so first normalize the direction: $\widehat v=(4,3)/5$. The <gradient> is
$$
\nabla f=-\frac{2(x,y)}{(1+x^2+y^2)^2},\qquad
\nabla f(1,0)=(-1/2,0).
$$
Taking the <dot product> with the <unit vector>,
$$
\boxed{D_{\widehat v}f(1,0)=(-1/2,0)\cdot(4/5,3/5)=-\frac25.}
$$
Using the unnormalized <vector> would instead give $-2$, the change per unit parameter along $(1,0)+tv$; it is not the rate per unit distance requested here.

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <product rule> gives the two <partial derivatives>
$$
g_x=x(2-x^2+y^2)e^{-(x^2+y^2)/2},\qquad
g_y=-y(2+x^2-y^2)e^{-(x^2+y^2)/2}.
$$
They both vanish at the origin. The <exponential function> is never zero, so any further <stationary point> must satisfy $x=0$ or $x^2-y^2=2$, and $y=0$ or $y^2-x^2=2$. Having both coordinates nonzero would require both opposite differences to be $2$, which is impossible. If $y=0$, the nonzero choices are $x=\pm\sqrt2$; if $x=0$, they are $y=\pm\sqrt2$. Hence the full list is
$$
\boxed{(0,0),\quad(\pm\sqrt2,0),\quad(0,\pm\sqrt2).}
$$

= c
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

At the origin the <Hessian matrix> is
$$
H_g(0,0)=\begin{pmatrix}2&0\\0&-2\end{pmatrix},\qquad\det H_g(0,0)=-4<0.
$$
Thus the <second-derivative test> gives a **<saddle point>**. Its opposite signs can also be checked without a test formula: $g(x,0)=x^2e^{-x^2/2}>0$ for $x\ne0$, whereas $g(0,y)=-y^2e^{-y^2/2}<0$ for $y\ne0$. Both types of values occur arbitrarily close to the zero value at the origin, excluding either a <local maximum> or a <local minimum>.

= 17
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Assume $L>0$. For any nonzero integer $k$,
$$
\int_{-L}^{L}\cos(k\pi x/L)\,dx
=\frac{L}{k\pi}[\sin(k\pi x/L)]_{-L}^{L}=0.
$$
For $m\ne n$, the product-to-sum identity expresses the integrand as one half the sum of cosines with nonzero frequencies $m-n$ and $m+n$, so the integral is zero. For $m=n>0$, use $\cos^2u=(1+\cos2u)/2$ to get $L$. Finally, for $m=n=0$ the integrand is one. The complete <Fourier orthogonality> formula is
$$
\boxed{\int_{-L}^{L}\cos\frac{m\pi x}{L}\cos\frac{n\pi x}{L}\,dx
=\begin{cases}2L,&m=n=0,\\L,&m=n\geq1,\\0,&m\ne n.\end{cases}}
$$
The constant mode has twice the squared norm of a positive-frequency <cosine> mode.

= b
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For period $2$, use the <Fourier series> convention
$$
f(x)\sim\frac{a_0}{2}+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}[a_n\cos(n\pi x)+b_n\sin(n\pi x)].
$$
The <Fourier coefficients> have prefactor $1$ on $[-1,1]$. First, $a_0=\int_{-1}^{1}e^x dx=2\sinh1$. For $q=n\pi$ the useful <antiderivatives> are
$$
\int e^x\cos(qx)dx=\frac{e^x[\cos(qx)+q\sin(qx)]}{1+q^2},\qquad
\int e^x\sin(qx)dx=\frac{e^x[\sin(qx)-q\cos(qx)]}{1+q^2}.
$$
At the endpoints $\sin(n\pi)=0$ and $\cos(n\pi)=(-1)^n$, so
$$
a_n=\frac{2(-1)^n\sinh1}{1+n^2\pi^2},\qquad
b_n=-\frac{2n\pi(-1)^n\sinh1}{1+n^2\pi^2}.
$$
Consequently, on $-1<x<1$,
$$
\boxed{e^x=\sinh1+2\sinh1\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}
\frac{(-1)^n[\cos(n\pi x)-n\pi\sin(n\pi x)]}{1+n^2\pi^2}.}
$$
The <Dirichlet-Jordan convergence theorem> justifies equality at every point of <continuity> of the periodic extension. At odd integers the extension has a <jump discontinuity> between $e$ and $e^{-1}$, so the series converges to their average $\cosh1$, regardless of any assigned endpoint value. This is the $\kappa=L=1$ case of the <Fourier series of a periodically extended exponential>.

= 18
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=18}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Under independent daily tosses, attending every lecture is the intersection of $24$ <independent events> of <probability> $3/4$. Hence
$$
\boxed{P(N=24)=\left(\frac34\right)^{24}\approx0.00100339.}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Attending none requires a tail on every independent toss. Thus
$$
\boxed{P(N=0)=\left(\frac14\right)^{24}=2^{-48}\approx3.55271\times10^{-15}.}
$$

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Write the count as a sum of $24$ <Bernoulli random variables>, one for each attendance indicator. Each <indicator random variable> has <expected value> $3/4$, so <linearity of expectation> gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[N]=24\cdot\frac34=18\ \text{lectures}.}
$$
Equivalently, $N$ has a <binomial distribution> with parameters $24$ and $3/4$.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

<Independence> makes the <variances> of the attendance indicators additive. The <binomial distribution> therefore gives $\operatorname{Var}(N)=np(1-p)=24(3/4)(1/4)=9/2$. Its positive <square root> is the <standard deviation>:
$$
\boxed{\sigma_N=\frac3{\sqrt2}\approx2.12132\ \text{lectures}.}
$$

= b
{parent=18}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Interpreting $f$ as the <probability density function> of the <random variable>, for $\alpha\leq\beta$ one has
$$
\boxed{P(\alpha\leq X\leq\beta)=\int_\alpha^\beta f(x)\,dx.}
$$
A single point has zero <probability>, so including or excluding either endpoint does not change this result.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

When the relevant <moments> are finite, the <mean> and <variance> are
$$
\boxed{\mu=\mathbb E[X]=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}x f(x)\,dx,\qquad
\sigma^2=\mathbb E[(X-\mu)^2]=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}(x-\mu)^2f(x)\,dx.}
$$
Equivalently, $\sigma^2=\int x^2f(x)\,dx-\mu^2$. The <mean> requires an integrable absolute first <moment>; finite second <moment> guarantees both quantities are finite.

= c
{parent=18}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Normalize the <probability density function> on its nonnegative support:
$$
1=A\int_0^\infty xe^{-\lambda x}\,dx=\frac A{\lambda^2}.
$$
The integral follows from one <integration by parts>, with the boundary term zero since $\lambda>0$. Hence **$A=\lambda^2$**. This is a <gamma distribution> of shape $2$ and rate $\lambda$.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Repeated <integration by parts> gives, for every nonnegative integer $j$,
$$
\int_0^\infty x^j e^{-\lambda x}dx=\frac{j!}{\lambda^{j+1}}.
$$
With $A=\lambda^2$, the first two <moments> are $\mathbb E[X]=\lambda^2(2!/\lambda^3)=2/\lambda$ and $\mathbb E[X^2]=\lambda^2(3!/\lambda^4)=6/\lambda^2$. Subtracting the square of the <mean> gives
$$
\boxed{\mu=\frac2\lambda,\qquad\sigma^2=\frac2{\lambda^2},\qquad\sigma=\frac{\sqrt2}{\lambda}.}
$$
If $X$ carries units, $\lambda$ has inverse units of $X$, so the <mean> and <standard deviation> have the units of $X$ and the <variance> has their square.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For $t\geq0$, evaluate the <survival function> directly:
$$
P(X>t)=\lambda^2\int_t^\infty xe^{-\lambda x}dx
=(1+\lambda t)e^{-\lambda t}.
$$
At $t=\mu+2\sigma=(2+2\sqrt2)/\lambda$, this becomes
$$
\boxed{P(X>\mu+2\sigma)=(3+2\sqrt2)e^{-2(1+\sqrt2)}\approx0.04662.}
$$
The parameter $\lambda$ cancels because the threshold scales with the distribution. Also $\mu-2\sigma<0$ lies outside the support, so in this particular case the two-sided event $|X-\mu|>2\sigma$ has the same <probability> as the stated upper-tail event.

= 19
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=19}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

**<Continuity>.** The <function> is <continuous> at $x_0$ when $\lim_{x\to x_0}f(x)=f(x_0)$. Equivalently, for every $\varepsilon>0$ there exists $\delta>0$ such that $|x-x_0|<\delta$ within the <domain of a function> implies $|f(x)-f(x_0)|<\varepsilon$; this is the <epsilon-delta definition of continuity>.

**<Differentiability>.** At an interior point $x_0$, the <difference quotient> must have a finite two-sided <limit of a function>:
$$
f'(x_0)=\lim_{h\to0}\frac{f(x_0+h)-f(x_0)}h.
$$
This defines <differentiability> and the <derivative>. At an endpoint one may instead state a one-sided definition relative to the <interval>. <Differentiability implies continuity>, because the increment is $h$ times a quotient with finite limit, but <continuity> alone does not guarantee <differentiability>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

As $x\to0$ through nonzero values, $1/x^2\to+\infty$ and $e^{-1/x^2}\to0=f(0)$. Thus $f$ is <continuous> at zero.

For the <derivative> at zero use its definition, rather than substituting into a formula obtained away from zero:
$$
f'(0)=\lim_{h\to0}\frac{e^{-1/h^2}}h=0.
$$
Indeed, putting $t=1/|h|$ makes the <absolute value> $te^{-t^2}$, which tends to zero since <Gaussian decay dominates every power>. Therefore **$f$ is <continuous> and <differentiable> at zero, with $f'(0)=0$**. Away from zero, the <chain rule> gives $f'(x)=2x^{-3}e^{-1/x^2}$.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For $x\ne0$, induction gives
$$
f^{(n)}(x)=P_n(1/x)e^{-1/x^2},\qquad
P_0(t)=1,\qquad P_{n+1}(t)=2t^3P_n(t)-t^2P_n'(t),
$$
where each $P_n$ is a <polynomial>. This recurrence follows from the <product rule> and $d(1/x)/dx=-1/x^2$. Since <Gaussian decay dominates every power>,
$$
\boxed{\lim_{x\to0}f^{(n)}(x)=0\quad\text{for every integer }n\geq0.}
$$
These limits also describe the <derivatives> of the extension at zero. If $f^{(n)}(0)=0$, its next <difference quotient> is $P_n(1/h)e^{-1/h^2}/h$, which again tends to zero. Induction proves existence and <continuity> of every <derivative>, all zero there. This is a <flat function>, covered more generally by <polynomial multiples of a Gaussian flat function>.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Insert $u=-1/x^2$ into the <exponential series> $e^u=\sum_{n=0}^\infty u^n/n!$. For every fixed $x\ne0$, this gives the expansion with <absolute convergence>
$$
\boxed{f(x)=\sum_{n=0}^\infty\frac{(-1)^n}{n!\,x^{2n}},\qquad a_n=\frac{(-1)^n}{n!}.}
$$
This is a <Laurent series> in inverse powers of $x$, as requested. It is not a <Maclaurin series> about zero: that series has every coefficient zero and cannot equal $f$ on a punctured neighborhood of zero. There is no contradiction, since the inverse-power expansion does not apply at $x=0$.

= v
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

The <flat function> $f$ is an <even function>, has its unique zero and <global minimum> at the origin, and approaches the horizontal <asymptote> $1$ as $x\to\pm\infty$. It decreases for negative $x$ and increases for positive $x$, meeting the origin with a horizontal <tangent line>.

Its <derivative> is an <odd function>: $f'(x)=2x^{-3}e^{-1/x^2}$ off zero, with $f'(0)=0$. It is negative to the left and positive to the right, tends to zero both at the origin and at infinity, and has one negative trough and one positive crest. Indeed $f''(x)=2e^{-1/x^2}(2-3x^2)/x^6$ away from zero, so the change in slope of $f'$ occurs once on each side. The exact crest and trough coordinates are not needed for the sketch.

\Image[paper-1-flat-function.png]
{title=An even smooth flat function and its odd derivative, both meeting zero with all derivatives zero}

= b
{parent=19}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A constant <sequence> must satisfy the <fixed point> equation $y_*=\sqrt{2+y_*}$. The <square root> forces $y_*\geq0$. Squaring gives $(y_*-2)(y_*+1)=0$, so the negative algebraic root is inadmissible. Hence
$$
\boxed{y_*=2.}
$$
Starting at $y_0=2$ produces the constant <sequence> $2,2,\ldots$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <cobweb plot> uses the <curve> $v=\sqrt{2+u}$ and the diagonal $v=u$. From a point on the diagonal, a vertical step to the <curve> computes the next iterate; a horizontal step returns its value to the diagonal.

For $0\leq u<2$, $(2+u)-u^2=(2-u)(1+u)>0$, so $u<\sqrt{2+u}<2$. Thus a start below $2$ gives a strictly increasing <sequence> bounded above by $2$. For $u>2$ the <inequalities> reverse to $2<\sqrt{2+u}<u$, giving a strictly decreasing <sequence> bounded below by $2$. Both types of staircase approach the same intersection.

The <bounded monotone sequence theorem> proves existence of a limit, and <continuity> then makes it a nonnegative <fixed point>. Part (i) gives
$$
\boxed{\lim_{n\to\infty}y_n=2\quad\text{for every }y_0\geq0.}
$$
This proves the <global convergence of a square-root iteration>; the graph below illustrates both cases without relying on a finite numerical sample as proof.

\Image[paper-1-square-root-cobweb.png]
{title=Iteration staircases approaching the positive fixed point from below and from above}

= 20
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Take positive dimensions measured in metres. The <volume> is $V=lwh$ and the external <surface area> constraint is $S=lw+2lh+2wh=12$. There is one base and four walls, with no lid. The <Lagrange multiplier> equations for $V-\lambda(S-12)$ are
$$
wh=\lambda(w+2h),\qquad lh=\lambda(l+2h),\qquad lw=2\lambda(l+w).
$$
The first equation implies $\lambda>0$. Multiply the first by $l$ and the second by $w$ and subtract; this gives $2\lambda h(l-w)=0$, hence $l=w=s$. The last equation gives $\lambda=s/4$. Substituting into the first yields $sh=(s/4)(s+2h)$ and therefore $s=2h$.

The constraint now reads $s^2+4sh=3s^2=12$. Thus
$$
\boxed{l=w=2\ \mathrm m,\qquad h=1\ \mathrm m,\qquad V_{\max}=4\ \mathrm{m^3}.}
$$
For a global check, apply the <arithmetic-geometric mean inequality> to $lw$, $2lh$ and $2wh$, whose sum is $12$. Their product is $4V^2\leq4^3=64$, so no feasible box has $V>4$. Equality holds exactly at the dimensions found above. This is the <maximum-volume open rectangular box at fixed surface area>.

= b
{parent=20}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <curve> has $x\ne0$ and can be written $y=1/x-x$. It is a <hyperbola> with vertical <asymptote> $x=0$ and oblique <asymptote> $y=-x$, crossing the horizontal axis at $(\pm1,0)$. It is symmetric under $(x,y)\mapsto(-x,-y)$.

On each branch $dy/dx=-1/x^2-1<0$. For $x>0$, the branch descends from $+\infty$ as $x\downarrow0$, passes through $(1,0)$, and approaches $y=-x$ from above as $x\to+\infty$. The negative branch follows by the central symmetry.

\Image[paper-1-hyperbola-nearest-point.png]
{title=The two hyperbola branches and their asymptotes, with the nearest point on the positive-horizontal branch marked}

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Minimize the squared distance $F=x^2+y^2$, which has the same minimizers as the distance. The <Lagrange multiplier> equations for the constraint $G=x^2+xy-1=0$ are
$$
2x=\lambda(2x+y),\qquad 2y=\lambda x,\qquad x^2+xy=1.
$$
The constraint <gradient> cannot vanish on this branch, since its second component is $x>0$. Eliminate $\lambda=2y/x$ to obtain $x^2=2xy+y^2$. Put $t=y/x$; then $t^2+2t-1=0$, so $t=-1\pm\sqrt2$. But the constraint says $x^2(1+t)=1$, requiring $1+t>0$ and selecting $t=\sqrt2-1$. It follows that
$$
\boxed{(x,y)=\left(2^{-1/4},(\sqrt2-1)2^{-1/4}\right),\qquad
 d_{\min}=\sqrt{2(\sqrt2-1)}.}
$$
To prove a <global minimum> on the whole $x>0$ branch, substitute $y=1/x-x$ into $F$:
$$
F=2x^2+x^{-2}-2\geq2\sqrt2-2.
$$
The <arithmetic-geometric mean inequality> proves this bound, with equality only when $2x^2=x^{-2}$, namely $x=2^{-1/4}$. Thus the <stationary point> is the unique closest point on the required branch. Numerically it is approximately $(0.840896,0.348311)$ and its distance is $0.910180$ in coordinate units.
